course: dictionary
lesson: 8215
title: Reunification: joining again, and whose word it is
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Reunification means joining together again something that had been split apart, especially a country that was divided into two. Break it into parts: re means again, union means joining, and -ation shows it is a noun for the act of doing it.
explain: Context gives you clues. «In 1939 Hitler and Stalin secretly split eastern Europe between them» tells you the land was divided, so reunification must mean putting it back together. The same word can also mean bringing a family back together after they were separated, as in «family reunification».
explain: Some words carry a point of view. In one example, the teacher still used the old Soviet word «reunification», but Danylo's grandmother remembered arrests and deportations, so for many families the same event felt like an occupation. When you meet this word, ask who is using it and what they think happened.
fact: Reunification is a noun, and it is usually uncountable, so we say «reunification», not «a reunification», in most sentences.
fact: Reunification means joining together again something that had been split.
fact: German reunification in 1990 joined East and West Germany after the Berlin Wall fell in 1989.
fact: «Family reunification» lets refugees bring relatives to join them.
fact: People can use the same word for one event and mean different things, so always check who is speaking.
exercise: «German reunification» happened in which year? | 1654 | 1989 | *1990 | 1939
exercise: What does reunification mean? | Moving house to a new town | Splitting something into two parts | Forgetting an old friend | *Joining together again something that had been split
exercise: What does «family reunification» let refugees do? | Leave their homes forever | Start a new business | *Bring relatives to join them | Forget their family names
test: Why might two people describe the same event with different words? | *They hold different views of what happened | They are too young to know history | One of them is lying about the date | The dictionary has changed
test: Which is the best example of reunification? | *Two halves of a divided country joining together again | A child learning to read | A family moving house to a new town | A country splitting into two
exam: Which word means joining together again something that had been split? | Alliance | *Reunification | Separation | Division
